# GitHub CLI and activity-record writes

Cursor's default sandbox can make `gh` report an invalid token even when
the host login is valid. That is not evidence the credential is broken.

- Run `gh` (auth, issue, pr, api, run) with `required_permissions: ["all"]`
  on the first attempt. Do not probe auth in the sandbox first.
- Before reporting activity-record as `unavailable` or falling back to
  `core-filesystem`, retry the same `gh` command unsandboxed.
- Do not write "token invalid" or "gh unavailable" into a handoff from a
  sandboxed check.
- Canonical plan and task status stay independent of whether GitHub
  publish succeeds.

```bash
# ❌ BAD — sandbox probe, then treat failure as missing auth
gh auth status

# ✅ GOOD — first GitHub call is already unsandboxed
# Shell required_permissions: ["all"]
gh auth status
gh issue create --repo owner/repo ...
```
